.Dd May 28, 2026
.Dt XTC_RCU 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_rcu_init ,
.Nm xtc_rcu_fini ,
.Nm xtc_rcu_read_lock ,
.Nm xtc_rcu_read_unlock ,
.Nm xtc_rcu_retire ,
.Nm xtc_rcu_synchronize ,
.Nm xtc_rcu_current_epoch
.Nd epoch-based read-copy-update
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.In xtc_rcu.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_rcu_init "void"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_rcu_fini "void"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_rcu_read_lock "void"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_rcu_read_unlock "void"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_rcu_retire "void *p" "xtc_rcu_free_fn fn"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_rcu_synchronize "void"
.Ft uint64_t
.Fn xtc_rcu_current_epoch "void"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm xtc_rcu
is an epoch-based read-copy-update primitive for read-mostly data
structures with deferred reclamation.  Readers operate
.Em wait-free
inside critical sections delimited by
.Fn xtc_rcu_read_lock
and
.Fn xtc_rcu_read_unlock .
Writers atomically swap pointers to new state and call
.Fn xtc_rcu_retire
to free the old state once all readers that may have observed it
have exited their critical sections.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_rcu_synchronize
blocks until all currently-active readers have exited.  Used by
writers that want to free old state synchronously rather than via
the deferred-retire path.
Any number of threads and fibers may call
.Fn xtc_rcu_synchronize
at once: each call advances the epoch by exactly one, and frees only
what its own grace period covers.
.Pp
The read side's first
.Fn xtc_rcu_read_lock
publishes its epoch and then runs a sequentially consistent fence, so
the pointer loads that follow cannot be reordered ahead of the publish
(about 9 ns on x86-64; nested calls and
.Fn xtc_rcu_read_unlock
take none).
.Pp
The implementation tracks per-reader epoch counters in a registered
linked list: one per OS thread, and one per fiber when the reader runs
on a fiber, because a fiber may yield, park, or migrate to another
loop inside a read-side critical section.
A fiber looks its counter up under a lock on its first
.Fn xtc_rcu_read_lock
and keeps it, so later read-side sections on the fiber take no lock.
Retired records are stamped with their epoch;
records two epochs old are guaranteed safe to free.
.Pp
Out of memory,
.Nm xtc_rcu
never frees an object under a reader.
A retirement that cannot be recorded is leaked instead, and reported as
an
.Dv XTC_TAIL_LIFECYCLE_DROP
event of
.Xr xtc_tail 3 .
A read-side that cannot get its per-reader counter still holds off
.Fn xtc_rcu_synchronize
until it ends.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_rcu_current_epoch
returns the current value of the global epoch counter.  It is
intended for diagnostics and tests -- the counter advances on each
grace period.
.Sh CHOOSING vs. xtc_lrlock
Both give wait-free reads.  Trade-offs:
.Bl -tag -width "xtc_lrlock" -offset indent
.It Vt xtc_rcu
Pointer-swap; arbitrary structure shape; deferred reclamation.
Best when the structure is small or rare-write.
.It Vt xtc_lrlock
Two-copy + replay; suits structures where the data is updated in
place
.Pq buffer pools, page caches .
Memory is 2x; reclamation is immediate via the publish-drain.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_lrlock 3 ,
.Xr xtc_lwlock 3 ,
.Xr xtc 7
.Sh HISTORY
Appeared in xtc 0.1.
Releases before 2.1.7 could free a retired object while a reader still
held it: when two or more calls of
.Fn xtc_rcu_synchronize
were in flight at once, from threads or from fibers on one loop or
several, and, even with a single writer, when a reader and a writer ran
on different OS threads.
